TI  - Earthworm assemblages of selected urban biotopes during land-use changes: abandoned allotments and agricultural areas in Dorsten
AU  - Keplin, B.
AU  - Broll, G.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - In order to characterise urban green spaces, several groups of fauna are usually mapped and analysed. In this context the investigation of earthworms is ofen neglected. However, this can provide useful information about urban areas under different management practices and at different successional stages. In this study, the lumbricides (species, life forms, abundance, biomass) of four study sites in the town of Dorsten were investigated. Additionally, physical and chemical soil properties were measured. Two sites had been used before as gardens, while the other two sites had been farmed. Today, they show different stages of succession. On the abandoned garden sites, abundances of lumbricides and biomass were higher. At a birch and willow dominated site, only epigeic species occurred. Further, lead emissions in urban sites play an important role in the contamination of urban soils. High biological activity, high humus content, low soil acidity and easily decomposable litter has been proven effective in preventing lead mobilisation. Therefore, soil organisms and their activity should be promoted by proper management of urban green spaces. DA
