TI  - Species/plot turnovers from repeated atlas mapping of breeding birds in southern Berlin, 1980 and 1990
AU  - Witt, K.
T2  - Acta Ornithologica
AB  - A breeding bird atlas was constructed for Berlin (West) based on field work conducted in 1977-1983 and repeated in 1989-1991. Surveys were performed within an area of 9000 ha. using a grid size of approximately 100ha. Species turnover-rates for the 90 plots averaged 22.8 % ±6 % and was generally similar among all habitat types studied (block buildings, high-rise buildings, industrial zone, residential area, parks/cemeteries). However, mean number of species per plot increased in the same order as habitats from 18.1±4.1 up to 32.2±4.6. The change of a farmland plot to a reconstructed park resulted in a nearly 50% change in species composition. Species dispersal and plot turnover-rates were analyzed in relation to patterns in plot occupancy; resident species and short-distance migrants tended to show a greater positive relationship to distributional pattern than long-distance migrants. A striking exception to this relationship occurred with the Crested Lark Galerida cristata where the relationship broke down for the Berlin population. A similar situation developed in other parts of Central Europe. But the Song Thrush Turdus philomelos, a short-distance migrant, suffered differential losses in the urban environment as related to the stability of surrounding forests. DA
