TI  - The kestrel (Falco tinnunculus L.) in Berlin: Feeding ecology along an urban gradient
AU  - Kübler, S.
AU  - Zeller, U.
T2  - Erde
AB  - Urbanisation, i.e. the expansion of cities and settlement area, has been increasing for a long time, both worldwide and in Germany. This process is often connected with an ecological degradation and a decimation of the fauna, a substantial decrease in species richness. On the other hand, the urban environment offers new opportunities. A lot of animal species adapt to the new urban living conditions. One example is the kestrel (Falco tinnunculus L.) which gets along well in big cities, e.g. in Rome (Salvati et al. 1999), in Warsaw (Rejt 2001) or in Berlin (Kupko et al. 2000). Urban kestrels can show a number of ecological and ethological differences in comparison with populations inhabiting natural habitats. In this study, the investigation focuses on the kestrel's feeding ecology as, beside the supply of nesting sites, the nutritional base is the most determining factor for the settlement and the survival of this species in a given environment. DA
