AU  - Keil, P.
AU  - Brosch, B.
AU  - Buch, C.
T2  - Natur und Landschaft
AB  - The Ruhr area is Europe's third largest economic region and the largest agglomeration in Germany. The decline of the mining and steel-producing industries has resulted in brownfield sites with a total area of more than 10,000 ha. The open urban-industrial brownfields of the Ruhr metropolis, with their large variety of target species and indicator species of urban and industrial landscapes, have nationwide importance for the preservation of biodiversity. Therefore, in cooperation between the Regionalverband Ruhr (local authority management group of the Ruhr district) and the Biologische Station Westliches Ruhrgebiet (biological station of the western Ruhr area) a research and development project was conducted with support from the German Federal Agency for Nature Conservation (BfN) with the aim to identify important brownfield sites in the Ruhr area. A methodology has been developed for selecting brownfields with the highest conservation potential. This two-stage approach, which is based on structural biotic and socio-economic evaluation criteria, can be also used in other urban agglomerations. DA
