377.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Characterisation of fish species diversity in waterways and urban waters
AU  - Wolter, C.
AU  - Vilcinskas, A.
T2  - Wasser und Boden
AB  - Between 1992 and 1997 the fish assemblage structure of 27 waterways of the north-east German lowlands was studied. The waterways surveyed show various states of anthropogenic alteration, from predominately natural, regulated rivers to urban waters and canals. Our aim was to characterise the influence of urbanisation on fish diversity in waterways by comparative analyses of fish assemblages. A total of 132 391 fish of 32 species were collected. The number of fish species in the various waterways ranged from 6 to 28 (mean 16.3 ± 5.3), Shannon's species diversity H' from 1.02-2.20 (1.67 ± 0.28), and the corresponding evenness was 0.51-0.76 (0.61 ± 0.08). The community dominance index (CDI) varied between 45.1% and 91.4% (66.9% ± 10.8%). Eight species, present in more than 90% of the waterways studied, were defined as reference species of the typical waterway-fish community: roach Rutilus rutilus, perch Perca fluviatilis, common bream Abramis brama, eel Anguilla anguilla, bleak Alburnus alburnus, silver bream Abramis bjoerkna, ruffe Gymnocephalus cernuus and ide Leuciscus idus, with roach and perch dominating. The comparison of urban and non-urban waterways showed significantly lower fish species numbers and fish diversity in urban waters: 13.7 ± 4.8 species, H' = 1.56 ± 0.25 compared to 18.8 ± 4.9 species, H' = 1.78 ± 0.27. There were no significant differences in terms of the evenness (0.61 ± 0.07 and 0.61 ± 0.08) or the community dominance index (69.9% ± 11.8% and 64% ± 9.8%). Both findings indicate that the higher fish diversity in the non-urban waterways resulted from their higher fish species inventory. Significant differences between urban and non-urban waterways were also found by comparing the relative abundances of perch, roach, common bream, and pike. Perch and common bream were significantly (p < 0.05) more abundant in the urban waterways, roach and pike significantly more (p < 0.01) in the non-urban waterways. A further loss of fish diversity in waterways must be expected with increasing urbanisation. DA
