AB  - Studies on the contribution of the yellow-necked mouseApodemus flavicollisand of other vertebrates to the diet of the tawny owlStrix alucowere carried out in Warsaw (central Poland) in the years 1983-2018. The frequency of the yellow-necked mouse in owl pellets increased at the break of the 20th and 21st centuries, particularly in some woodlands in the peri-urban area of the agglomeration. In the second half of the first decade of the twenty-first century, this mouse species colonised a park in the city centre. The yellow-necked mouse may become an important competitor for the synurbic population of the striped field mouseApodemus agrarius. DA
