465.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Rhine as recreational area
AU  - Berger, W.
T2  - WASSER ENERGIEWIRTSCH.
AB  - The author is concerned with the German part of the River Rhine and its recreational potential. While the upper and lower parts of the Rhine are primarily local recreation areas, the middle part has developed into a truly national and international tourist centre. The upper reaches of the Rhine, in particular, the old Rhine, offer an abundance of wildlife and plenty of scope for all kinds of watersports. The local authorities are constantly developing the untouched parts of the old Rhine and artificial lakes by improving access to them. While passenger boat traffic on the upper Rhine is almost exclusively concerned with local needs, it is of prime importance for the tourist trade along the middle Rhine. This part of the Rhine is favorably situated between the two main population centers in Germany. Greater mobility of the population has led to such an increase in tourism that some towns are almost completely dependent on it. While swimming in the Rhine is almost impossible because of pollution, there are excellent swimming pools to be found all along the Rhine. The number of small boats on the river increases steadily as well as the camping areas along its banks. Pollution is gradually being brought under control through the greater use of clearing installations. DA
