AB  - For this investigation the roe deer bags of hunting areas in 53 districts and towns of North Rhine-Westphalia in the period from 1987/1988 to 1994/1995 were used (Fig. 2). These hunting bags, together with 6 variables of land use and their percentage on the hunting area, were put in factor analysis for each of the 8 hunting seasons. In each analysis 3 characteristic features (factors) were found out. These 3 factors of land form stand for: factor 1 = highlands, factor 2 = lowlands, factor 3 = fertile plains (as shown in tables 1 to 8). Highlands (low mountain range): The variables of land use with highest share on the landscape are woodland and pasture land. The positive influence of the woodland onto the roe deer density decreases until 1992/1993, in the same period however, pasture land show an increase of influence on the roe deer density. On the one hand, grain and corn fields have a negative valuation for the influence on roes, but the valuations decline until the hunting season of 1992/1993 - obviously an improvement for the roe deer takes place. On the other hand, the increasing negative values of root crops do not say much for the development of the roe deer density. The very high but decreasing results of roe deer are a sign for one origin of beginning problems in those habitats. Lowlands: The results of grain and corn lands, with an increasingly positive influence in the eight-year period were of prime importance for the determination of the second factor. The statistics of traffic areas are marked very low and therefore indicate the disadvantages for roes. In the regions of this landscape we detect by the roe deer values an increase of these animals. Fertile plains: The determination of this region, on the left bank of the Rhine, is highly dependent on the root crop variable. The results for the woodland show favourable influences on the roes, just as the decreasing negative results of pasture lands and maize fields that obviously have a suitable trend of influence. The traffic areas describe, like in factor 1, a growing pessimum on the roe deer. DA
