511.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Origin of the highly enriched sb-abundances in fine-dust
AU  - Weckwerth, G.
T2  - Antimony: Characteristics, Compounds and Applications
AB  - The mean concentration of antimony (Sb) in the upper continental crust (UCC) is with 0.2 ppm nearly 10 times lower than that of the far better known and stronger observed element arsenic (As). Since the comparable toxic element Sb is in the fine dust (specifically from towns) between 2 and 4 times more abundant than As, it needs a higher environmental attention. Like no other element Sb belongs in both the coarse grained (2.5-10μm) as well as in the fine grained aerosols (< 2.5μm) to the most highly enriched elements. This proofs presence at least of one widespread anthropogenic Sb-source, which is mechanically produced from a solid and one by condensation from a gas component. After control and disappearance of much higher Sb-rich-components from industry in the 80ties the source of the remained coarse grained component was found soon to come from traffic, according to measured gradients with the distance to frequented streets. Final evidence was taken from a wide-spread chemical fingerprint found in the coarse grained fraction of urban fine dust (Weckwerth, 2001). The constant ratios of concentration between the elements Sb, Cu and Mo could be verified as the mean ratios of the mainly in Germany 1997 used brake-linings. 10 years later the same ratios could be still measured with a far better statistic figure in a frequently used tunnel at Cologne. Additionally, a new element could be taken in the chemical fingerprint with tin (Sn), according to a new type of brake lining using Sn-sulfid instead of Sb-sulfide as fine-lubricant. For the first time the measurement in a tunnel 2006 shows an increased enrichment of Sn, according to almost 30% German market share this Sn-rich type of brake lining has reached during 10 years. Meanwhile, similar fingerprints have been measured and used as tracers of traffic emission in many countries, confirming a similar use of Sb-sulfide and a strong dominance of this kind of traffic emissions for the given elements in the coarse grained fraction (2.5-10μm) of the fine dust. In a similar way it could be shown, that also a strong proportion of trains use Sb-rich brake-linings (Weckwerth, 2010). © 2012 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved. DA
