539.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - The development of the aquatic biotope in the river Bode after industrial and natural salinization in the area of the community of staßfurt from 1992 till 1995 - District Aschersleben - Staßfurt/Federal State Sachsen-Anhalt (Germany)
AU  - Tappenbeck, L.
T2  - Limnologica
AB  - The river Bode and its tributary streams drain the eastern part of the Harz mountains. The river is polluted by municipal, agricultural and industrial dumpings. Intensive auto-purification processes are induced in the body of water and in the biotopes in certain sections along the course of the river. Intensive processes of salinisation in the area of the Staßfurt community cause contrary changes, especially destruction of flora and fauna in the river. The main enterprise responsible can only Sodawerke Staßfurt which causes the high concentration of salt in the river Bode downstream of the area of Staßfurt community, whilst geogene influences are also assumed. In 1990, Sodawerke Staßfurt caused the chloride pollution of the river Saale near Groß Rosenburg at 13.4% and the chloride pollution of the river Elbe near Magdeburg at 10.4% (SCHÜBEL 1992). An important parameter for the assessment of salinization of water resources is the concentration of chlorides. Degrees of concentration vary enormously according to the different water levels of the rivers making difficult the development of an intact aquatic biotope. In the river Bode near Neugattersleben, the concentration of chlorides varied from 2670 mg · 1-1 (im August 1992) to 340 mg · 1-1 (im December 1992). In 1994, a decline of the avarage chloride concentration was observed towards 60% compared with the 1990's concentration. In 1995, a slight increase was observed again. In comparison to these figures, here can be only a small concentration of chlorides at the fixed sample points in Egeln (Nord) and Staßfurt: These portions of chlorides are caused by tributary streams which are polluted by salt, i.e. the tributary streams of the river Großer Graben upriver Oschersleben as well as tributaries of Ehle and Marbe between Egeln (Nord) and Staßfurt. The karst area of the Marbe and the soda slagheaps are built up by products and waste water of the Staßfurt sodafactory. The intensive processes of salinization and the high concentration of nutrieats caused by waste dumps and assumed geogene influences within the city of Staßfurt and downriver have been reduced since 1990. Since 1992 species of macrozoobenthos have been found again in formerly completely deserted sections along the course of the river Bode and the numbers of benthic invertebrats species has increased. The increase of the number of species and individuals, and the whole resuscitation of the aquatic biotope, promise a positive further development. The right river bank was resettled up to the town of Hohenerxleben. Every year the number of macrozoobenthos became higher, several species of fish have been observed again. But the proportions per area are very small and differ from season to season and depend on the water level. Nearly all demanding species are missing and salt-prefering species can be found a lot. Restrictions and modernizations, step by step, as well as re-placing of dumpings, would help to improve the development of flora and fauna in the water. A reshabing of the structure of water resources partly forced by man, e.g. controlled plantation or restricted technological measures for maintaining the river course can influence, positively, the settlement of benthic water organisms. In the future, not only the water resources and their use should be considered, but also the river as a complex unit of waterbody, bottom, substratum, banks, river floot plains and the tributary streams as well as all their interrelationsships. DA
