AB  - Recolonization of a newly restored stream within the industrial conurbation "Ruhrgebiet' was studied. The shift from a closed masonry to an open pebble substrate increases both number of taxa and individuals of stream invertebrates. Unspoiled affluents function as donor streams for missing parts of the biocoenosis. Main destructions caused by stormwater outlets are substrate silting as well as substrate relocations depending on extent and duration of discharge events, eutrophication, warming and probably oxygen-depletion. Recolonization follows the expected patterns: initially a quick peak development of opportunistic species as Gammaridae, Chironomidae and Simuliidae being abundant in very high numbers and then with the formation of new habitats like the interstitial, leaf packs, rooted plants etc., more specialized taxa as shredders (eg Nemoura cinerea, Tipula spp), algae scrapers (eg Baetis spp) or predators (eg Rhyacophila nubila, Plectrocnemia conspersa), forming a less individual rich but more stable community. -from English summary DA
