AB  - Densities of macroinvertebrates and diet compositions of stone loach, Barbatula barbatula, and gudgeon, Gobio gobio, were determined at two locations on the Glems River, Baden-Württemberg, Germany, in March and May 1995. The two sites were located downstream and upstream of a former mill weir, which had recently been replaced by a "boulder ramp" in order to restore longitudinal continuity. Mean densities of macroinvertebrates ranged between 8000 and 10 700 organisms m-2, with Chironomidae, Oligochaeta, Ephemeroptera, and Trichoptera being the dominant groups. Densities generally did not significantly differ between sites, but abundances of Ephemeroptera and Trichoptera decreased between March and May. Both fish species fed on all dominant taxa present in the benthos, except oligochaetes, and showed a strong preference towards chironomids. No dietary differences could be observed between upstream and downstream sites and both species showed high diet overlaps which increased between March and May. The feasibility of this type of approach for monitoring anthropogenic disturbances of river continuity is discussed. DA
