680.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - News. Building Bridges - Fulfilling the Potential for Gas in the 21st Century
AU  - Watts, P.
T2  - Petrole et Techniques
AB  - Gas offers abundance, cleanliness, and flexibility. Expanding its use is the best medium term response to the threat of climate change. There are challenges to building the markets for gas and the systems to deliver it over increasing distances. Gas projects require financial, technological, and commercial strengths and cooperation between government and business. In the past 30 yr the gas industry has responded to these challenges through pipeline projects and the development of LNG. A number of projects, e.g., the Sakhalin 2 investment announced in May 2003, illustrate the work now underway to ensure that gas fulfills its potential across the world. Shell, Mitsui, and Mitsubishi launched the Sakhalin project 2 wk prior to the 22nd World Gas Congress. The Sakhalin 2 project increases diversity and offers competitive advantages particularly from its closeness to market. Long-standing customers of Shell such as Tokyo Gas and Tokyo Electric share this assessment. The Sakhalin development opens the way for Russian gas supplies to Pacific markets. The vision of the Russian government has been vital to make possible the Sakhalin development. At 4.8 million ton each, the two Sakhalin trains will be among the largest constructed. Sakhalin Energy is aware of its responsibilities for the unique and diverse environment onshore and offshore Sakhalin Island. A consortium of Chiyoda and Toyo Engineering from Japan with KhinEnergo and Nipigaspererabotka from Russia has won a contract of ∼ $2.5 billion to build the LNG plant. Russian industry should undertake about 70% of the work over the lifetime of the project. In China, the West-East pipeline is costing $4 billion to construct. LNG is a competitive way to commercialize stranded gas resources. With experience from the Shell Middle Distillate plant in Bintulu, Shell plans much larger second generation plants with a focus on the Middle East. GTL products could be blended with diesel motor fuel or used directly. Shell is now selling a GTL/diesel blend in Thailand and has launched trials with Volkswagen in Berlin. Trials are also planned in Tokyo, London, and California. A number of other related developments and observations are discussed. DA
