690.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Pyramid på Gustav Adolfs torg
AU  - Milsson, S.
T2  - Konsthistorisk Tidskrift
AB  - The years around 1780 are significant ones in Scandinavian art history. At this time a number of architects travelled south to France and Italy, where they came into direct contact with international art development and its revival of Classical An tiquity. Among the Swedes were Eric Palmstedt (later on the master architect of Gustaf Ill's theatre in Gripsholm Castle) and Olof Tempelman (later active at Haga), and among the Danes, Nicolas Jardin's best pupil, Peter Meyn. In addition to these travellers, there was still another Swede, Carl August Ehrensvärd. In 1780 he was thirty‐five years old, a colonel in the army's fleet and actually somewhat of a dilettante in the field of architecture. However, this Italian trip was to mean equally as much for him as for the professional architects. His writings on art theory, which he published later, “Italian Journey” and “Philosophy of Fine Arts” (both 1786) and the numerous drawings and projects, inspired by the Italian sojourn, indicate a very advanced comprehension of art. As to his architecture, this was of Continental style and fully as radical as that of the French Utopists. Ehrensvärd arrived in Italy in the autumn of 1780 and dedicated himself to the modern, “austere” phase of Neo‐Clas‐sicism. Typically characteristic of his viewpoint was his admiration for some Egyptian sculptures and for the Doric architecture of Paestum and Agrigentum. Ehrensvärd has described his visit to Paestum in a drawing, which shows how the temple was measured (fig. 1), and in a letter, where he relates how he argued with a Frenchman, Neufville, on the Grecian Doric Order with such success that the Frenchman “would have liked to cut off the bases of all Roman columns.” As Emil Kaufmann showed, the art of building during the last decades of the eighteenth century was characterized by aspirations toward simplicity and elementary forms. Piranesi's spectacular etchings and engravings of Roman Antiquity were also inspiring. These features are clearly reflected in Ehren‐svärd's theories, drawings and building projects. His theory of art recalls that of Francesco Milizia; it is just as heterogeneous, both traditional and modern at the same time. This is borrowed from the Vitruvian log‐cabin doctrine. Nevertheless, when he explains himself more in detail, it is in accordance with the aesthetics of the day: (What is the nature of the tree?)—the straight line, the square and the circular column of the trunk. A square house is beautiful, he says in another connection, and also recommends low roofs. He endorses his views in some drawings (fig. 2). The attraction of a visionary architecture such as Piranesi's, or what I call here an archi‐tectura gigas is indicated also in a drawing, a paraphrase on Roman architecture (fig. 3). Just as in Piranesi's fantastic landscapes, human figures are here transformed into symbols of measurement, marking the scale. While he was abroad, Ehrensvärd no doubt came into contact with extreme architecture also. During the winter of 1779 ‐80 in Rome, Eric Palmstedt had copied some drawings by Claude Thomas Lussault for a mausoleum (fig. 4). This project—preserved solely in Palmstedt's copies and to date unknown to international experts—was one of the earliest in the group of Neo‐Classicism's imaginary pyramid constructions. It portrays perhaps the first pyramid of “ideal” type: the cross‐section is about as an equilateral triangle (Dr. Oscar Reuter‐svärd's researches). It is probable that Ehrensvärd studied such drawings as these, if not in Rome then on his way north in Bologna, where he met the Danish architect Peter Meyn. About 1780 Meyn had been occupied with studies and plans for a project with a pyramid as central component. Five of these suggestions are preserved. As a whole they adhere to Peyre's model designs for burial places, but are considerably smaller in size (fig. 5). Concerning the central body of the buildings, these have other prototypes: Nicolas Jardin, Meyn's professor at the Royal Academy in Copenhagen, had composed for King Frederic V a castrum doloris in this form, Neo‐Classicism's first stereometrically pure pyramid; in its size, in its type and even in its surface structure Meyn's pyramid resembles another monument, the mausoleum Pyramid of Caius Ces‐tius at Porta Paola in Rome. The region around there by the Aurelian Wall had been used as a Protestant cemetery since the middle of the eighteenth century, as depicted on Nolli's map of 1748. Burials in this graveyard took place at night by torchlight (fig. 6). It is possible that such stage effects acted as an inspiring stimulant for artists and architects. Here existed not only a pyramid form, available for use, but also a large amount of atmosphere and tools for the imagination. The components I have now pointed out, the desire for elemental form, the meeting with Peter Meyn in Bologna, the romanticism around the Pyramid of Cestius, etc., were all combined in some building projects Ehrensvärd produced in Berlin on the way home from Italy. Ehrensvärd arrived in Berlin towards the end of July 1782. There he met his cousin, Gustav Johan Ehrensvärd, who was the Swedish minister in the diplomatic corps in Berlin, and who could tell him important news: Sweden's Queen Dowager Louisa Ulrica had died July the 16th. Carl August Ehrensvärd had formerly in a letter to his cousin expressed his wishes concerning a radical change in the central parts of Stockholm towards a classical style. The notification of the Queen's death now gave him grounds for new designs for her funeral arrangements. He was occupied with this project from two points of view: ideas for a temple with columns and for a pyramid. In both cases the edifice contains a castrum doloris. This is constructed as an open Doric temple (fig. 7), and was conceived as the central point in a vast structure. On the back side of the same drawing Ehrensvärd has drawn the whole plan for the mausoleum (fig. 8). A few figures placed in the castrum indicate the scale; it is a large‐scaled, heavily‐proportioned architecture. Still the proportions are considerably intensified in the other plans, and a veritable architectura gigas can be declared here. The castrum is now placed in a vaulted, architectural frame which shuts out the daylight. People, barely small dabs, are grouped in front of the black temple, which is illuminated with torches and candle‐flames (fig. 9). In another drawing this interior is incorporated in a huge pyramid (fig. 10). In a visionary pen and wash drawing (fig. 11) Ehrensvärd rendered the exterior of the pyramid, its situation in the city plan, and the funeral march in defile past the Queen lying in state. He placed an arch of triumph at Norrbro and erected his black pyramid on Gustav Adolf's Square, opposite the Royal Palace. A sea of people fill the open square and a procession with torches marches past the arch of triumph toward the pyramid. A row of torchbearers stand along the pyramid's foreside: it seems to rest on a pedestal of flames. Ehrensvärd's triumphal arch does not follow the Roman pattern; neither does it revert to Palladio's nor Blondel's prototypes. For the most part it resembles Ledoux's “sunken” architecture—his arch in front öf Maison de Thélusson in Paris had been erected in 1780, and it was soon studied by Swedish architects. Ehrensvärd should have seen it during his stay in Paris on his way to Italy. Architecturally, his pyramid on the open square was equally as radical. The sources of inspiration cited above, Lussault's and Meyn's suggestions for mausoleums, seem already out‐of‐date in comparison with this uncompromising structure. The podium, porticoes and colonnades have been eliminated. Remaining is only a product of pure stereometry, an ideally formed pyramid placed directly on the flat ground. Ehrensvärd's mighty architecture was never executed in actual materials. While he was occupied with his visions in Berlin, the Queen Dowager was buried in Stockholm. The importance of his project lies on an æsthetic level. The triumphal arch and the pyramid are powerful expressions for a Northern Style of architecture. In contrast to many contemporary architects, Ehrensvärd consciously sought for national principles for his Classicism, and he discovered that they must aim at a great simplicity. The Archaic periods of Egypt and Greece served as patterns in this respect. But the Swedish climate and landscape required even more emphatic forms: “we should have such strong simplicity that it becomes out of proportion. It became caricature.” In his philosophy of art Ehrensvärd followed the traditional demands for unity, symmetry and moderation; like Vitruvius, Perrault and Blondel, he also stressed that proportions constitute the true foundations of art. Taste. … “lies in the regularity of spirit and form, in the feeling for the beautiful, in other words in the proportions.” But when he sought a Northern Style, he violated these laws and converted the classic forms in the direction of their antithesis: style and character passed into caricature. Thus, we are concerned with an ambivalence which makes it exceedingly difficult to judge Ehrensvärd's art. And finally, how are we to interpret such architecture as the arch and pyramid on Gustav Adolf's Square— isn't it just as contradictory? Actually, the problem has a substantial background. In Italy Ehrensvärd had at an early stage become aware of the special difficulties due to arise when Neo‐Classicism's ideas were transplanted to a northern milieu. In Pompeii he found that frost had caused mosaic floors to break in pieces: “This is a picture of how it would be in Sweden. No, no art for Sweden. Only news‐sheet writers, apothecaries and ministers should be found there. The cold doesn't hurt them, and that is the only abundance we can boast of.” The journey north made him equally as depressed, and time after time he made notes of similar, negative observations. After his return home to Sweden, he wrote: “I feel bitter hate for us all, if I cannot have the lift of a pair of white gloves and a bottle of porter—two columns on which I intend to build my whole architecture.” Ehrensvärd usually tested his theories in drawings and plans. Consequently, this negative point of view on the arts also came to light. Simultaneously with the project for the Queen's burial, he drew suggestions for an “Egyptian Temple” on Gustav Adolfs's Square (fig. 12). This design makes an unreal impression in its objectively rendered surroundings and should probably be seen in connection with the above quotation; its intention is to make clear the impossibility of a Neo‐Classic architecture in Sweden. This may be established with even greater certainty in regard to another sketch, in which the fir trees and huts of the North figure as pitiable accessories in front of the grandiose vision of “Egyptian” architecture (fig. 13). After his arrival in Sweden in the autumn of 1782, Ehrensvärd devoted himself to military missions and was inly active as an architect in a couple of cases. In a park on an estate in central Sweden he built a complete copy, in full scale of the so‐called “Theseion” Temple in Athens. It was the first copy of this kind in Europe (about 1785). Ehrensvärd was conscious of this, but at the same time he payed tribute to the eclecticism of the day. When he transplanted a whole classic building to Swedish environment, he denied his own ideas for an original Northern Style. The architecture Ehrensvärd actually executed conceals his innermost intentions. We would hardly be able to understand them, or even believe in this "Egyptian” architecture, if we were not able to study it in three dimensions, in some models which Ehrensvärd had ordered sculptured in wood. They are now in Karlskrona and were probably placed there during Ehrensvärd's period as Admiral of the Fleet. With short columns and smooth or fluted shafts, a tremendously massive architrave and a heavy tympanum, these models show a row of porticoes, possibly planned to be contructed in the dockyard (fig. 14). As an entrance to a building such as, for exemple, for martial work or administration, this architecture could be considered motivated—Ehrensvärd's friend and collaborator, af Chapman, chief director of the dockyard, had erected a similar portico with columns at his country place, but it was still Doric in its proportions. Seeking for counterparts to Ehrensvärd's architectural models, we find examples from Antiquity in buildings constructed before the Doric style had secured its final character—Apollo Temple in Syracuse and Artemis Temple in Corfu, both marked by an extraordinary heaviness (fig. 15, 16). Modern parallels appear with the French Utopists. Ledoux's design for a prison in Aix‐en‐Provence has four porticoes supported by this thick‐set, sturdy type. They were probably designed about the same time as Ehrensvärd's, about 1785. As an architect, Ehrensvärd was of slight practical importance. However, many of his theories and ideas for a Northern Style were followed through by Louis Jean De‐prez the internationally best known of “Swedish” artists at this time. Deprez's design for the stables at Haga is described by Kaufmann as “out of proportion” (Compare fig. 17). Inspired by the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus, he also planned a Pantheon in the form of a pyramid, which in its Baroque‐like construction constitutes a direct contrast to Ehrensvärd's radical architecture. © 1964 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC. DA
