T2  - Water Science and Technology
AB  - Pollution by total bacteria in the Moselle is heavy and uniform. Thermophilic amoebae distribution shows a statistical relation (probably not causal) to faecal pollution. Naegleria distribution appears related to temperature increases, especially downstream of power stations. The dominant species seem to be N. lovaniensis in early summer and N. australiensis in late summer. Pathogenic N. fowleri were not frequent in the river, nor abundant (maximum 20/100 l). They were found near power plants, but occasionally upstream. The river Moselle flows from France to Luxembourg and Germany to the Rhine in Koblenz. The Moselle valley is also an active industrial region of the North Eastern France concentrating several plants (foundry, steel and iron works, electricity power stations and so on ...) in the vicinity of towns: Toul, Pont a Mousson, Metz, Thionville. So the river receives the water heated by industrial plants and the aim of this study was to know the effect of the power plant's discharges on the presence and abundance of Naegleria, among other microbiological and chemical parameters. For this, studies have been carried out since 1986 on eighteen sampling stations along a one hundred and twenty kilometres stretch of the river, from Toul upstream to the Luxembourg border.; DA
