TI  - Anniversary in Hungary. Ten-year celebration at Ligno Novum. Hungary's forestry
T2  - Holz - Kurier
AB  - A report covers the 10th Anniversary Ligno Novum Trade Fair at Sopron, Hungary on August 23-26, 2000. The first Ligno Novum trade fair was held in 1991 with 45 exhibitors on 430 sq m of grounds in the sports hall at Sopron. The number of visitors was a scarce 200. The next Ligno Novum was the turning point with an increased number of attendees. The fair is a meeting point of wood experts such that it is supported jointly by the University of Soil Culture in Vienna and timber technology in Sopron. In 1995 the Wood Tech Forestry Fair that was an independent timber trade fair joined Ligno Novum. Since then the Ligno Novum fairgrounds have grown to 7,000 sq m and today there are over 15,000 visitors. The visitors come from Romania and the Czech Republic by the busloads and Austria is well represented. Hungary is quality conscious because technology from the West despite its cost is salable. At Leitz of Riedau, Austria, quality and engineering are honored. The company's five service stations in Hungary make company products more salable there. Also, the maintenance and machines and tools in Hungary because of the lower personnel costs are less costly than in Austria. The payment morale is good. The Weinig company of Tauberbischofsheim, Germany has a good order situation but according to T. Alpár, the East is open to new technology but small-scale cabinet makers low on capital have sought for more favorably priced used machines. The Baillou company of Wiener Neudorf, Austria offers decoratively veneered coreboard from the Czech Republic and laminated plywood. In Hungary, if boards are required for small pieces, the less costly plywood is fallen back upon. Hungary has 1.8 million hectares of forest that is 50:50 distributed between public and private ownership. About 8,000 hectares/yr are reforested. This number has almost doubled in the past two years. New forestation is mainly with domestic tree species. The Hungarian Forest Association, founded in 1866, arranged the forest exhibition at the Ligno Novum to demonstrate modern forest machines. DA
