TI  - How non-conventional feedstocks will affect aromatics technologies
AU  - Köhler, E.
T2  - Oil Gas European Magazine
AB  - The abundance of non-conventional feedstocks such as coal and shale gas has started to affect the availability of traditional base chemicals such as propylene and BTX aromatics. Although this trend is primarily fuelled by the fast growing shale gas economy in the US and the abundance of coal in China, it will cause the global supply and demand situation to equilibrate across the regions. Lower demand for gasoline and consequently less aromatics-rich reformate from refineries will further tighten the aromatics markets that are however expected to grow at healthy rates. Refiners can benefit from this trend by abandoning their traditional fuel-oriented business model and becoming producers of petrochemical intermediates, with special focus on paraxylene (PX). Cheap gas from coal (via gasification) or shale reserves is an advantaged feedstock that offers a great platform to make aromatics in a cost-competitive manner, especially in regions where naphtha is in short supply. Gas condensates (LPG and naphtha) are good feedstocks for paraffin aromatization, and methanol from coal or (shale) gas can be directly converted to BTX aromatics (MTA) or alkylated with benzene or toluene to make paraxylene. Most of today's technologies for the production and upgrading of BTX aromatics and their derivatives make use of the unique properties of zeolites. © 2014 URBAN-VERLAG Hamburg/Wien GmbH. DA
