861.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - The influence of drainage from potash mining on the composition of the algal flora in the River Wipper (Thuringia, Germany) - A preliminary study
AU  - Leya, T.
AU  - Kies, L.
T2  - Limnologica
AB  - In a preliminary study from September until November 1995 the saline River Wipper in Northern Thuringia, which is located in a former potash mining area, was investigated to characterize its algal flora. From three places near the towns of Worbis, Sollstedt and Hachelbich micro- and macroalgae were sampled from stones, mud, sand and the flowing water. One location on the very saline River Urbach in Ebeleben was also sampled once. Analyses of the species composition, especially of diatoms (Bacillariophyceae), were performed and halobic indices calculated. The chlorophyll a contents of micro- and macroalgae samples were used as a parameter for biomass. Chemical analyses of the river water were performed by "Staatliches Umweltamt Sondershausen". With increasing salinity downstream from Worbis and Sollstedt to Hachelbich the frequency of mesohalobic diatom species within the periphytic populations rose from 22% to 43% in disfavour of oligohalobic species (73% to 46%). Frequencies of polyhalobic species also increased from Worbis (4%) to Hachelbich (7%). The halobic index rose from 27 at Worbis and 29 at Sollstedt to 54 at Hachelbich. As for the biomass of benthic algae throughout all locations sampled, high chlorophyll a values were observed (Cladophora glomerata: max. 1700 mg · m-2, Entermorpha intestinalis: max. 460 mg · m-2 and epilithic microalgae: 100 ... 350 mg · m-2). Very low chlorophyll a values were observed in drift samples (0.02 ... 2.00 mg · m-2). The algal flora in the upper River Wipper, downstream at least as far as Sollstedt (max. chloride values 450 mg · l-1 during sampling period), can be described as a freshwater phytocenosis lacking haloxenic (salt intolerant) species. Downstream from Sollstedt a change in the composition of species is evident, characterized by an increase in the frequency of meso- and polyhalobic diatom species. DA
