918.
TY  - CHAP
TI  - The checkered reception of fairy-tale films in the germany of the brothers grimm
AU  - Zipes, J.
T2  - Fairy-Tale Films Beyond Disney: International Perspectives
AB  - This chapter explores Japanese fairy-tale films, in both live action and animated media. Fantasy in Japan, while it certainly has wish fulfilling and wondrous aspects, also engages with darker and more complex human conditions, such as loss, grief, and bittersweet melancholy. The realms of Japanese fantasy are rarely the lands of “happily ever after”. With its brooding vision of a devastated city gate and a murdered character who speaks from beyond the grave to tell his version of events, Rashomon is hardly a conventional fairy tale full of supernatural characters. Spirited Away is a richly detailed reminder of a lost pre-modern Japan, the bathhouse both emblemizes the richness of traditional Japanese culture and also its fragility. The Tale of the Princess Kaguya is a universally accessible tale of love and wonder. However, to a post-earthquake and tsunami Japan, the film offers a profoundly beautiful vision of the natural cycle of birth, growth, and death, and then, perhaps, rebirth. © 2016 Taylor & Francis. DA
