TY  - JOUR
TI  - Dynamics and diversity of invasive plant species in Germany
AU  - Loos, G.H.
AU  - Schmitt, T.
T2  - Geographische Rundschau
AB  - Studies of alien plant species in Germany started with a period of "adventive floristics": Investigations of the non-native flora of certain "arrival" places in urban-industrial areas were carried out (freight depots, harbours, mills, wool-carding shops, garbage dumps). Nowadays the research is focused on invasive neophytes. In former times most neophytes were xenophytic casuals, introduced by imports of cereal and root crops, seed, wool and other goods. Changes in the transport systems of goods led to a considerable decrease of xenophytes while the number of garden escapes increased. Almost all invasive species with impacts on ecosystems are former garden escapes. The "hotspots" of neophyte diversity are still situated in the urban-industrial regions because most neophytes are thermophilic and inhabit ruderal, disturbed biotopes. Additionally, disturbed riparian biotopes at the rivers are colonized by invasive neophytes to a great extent. Population dynamics of invasive species is not always a progressive process. After accomplishing saturation stage there could be a decrease of population size. Invasion dynamics is furthermore influenced by infraspecific (genetic) diversity of neophytes and hybridization processes which could lead to new taxa, more successful in invasion than their parental species. As every species implies its own dispersal history and dynamics, two examples of species with different "invasion paths" are pointed out. Canadian Waterweed (Elodea canadensis! as a species distributed in streams and other waters, but with population decreasing after a vast expansion in Europe; and South African Ragwort (Senecio inaequidens), an alien introduced by wool in former times, but with extensive spreading in the westernmost part of Germany within the last decades. DA
