1202.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Flora and browsing on wild fields on the farmlands in Lower Saxony
AU  - Aboling, S.
T2  - Zeitschrift fur Jagdwissenschaft
AB  - In order to clarify the question of the ecological value of annual wild fields in farmlands, the seeded crops and plant species (the latter according to Braun-Blanquet) on 68 sites in the lowlands and mountains of Lower Saxony were investigated during the vegetation period of 2002. Each wild field is subjected to an individual cultivation procedure; every second one (of which 26 are fallow) was seeded to a different crop. The spectrum of wild plant species ranges from field wild herb associations to Ruderal mugwort associations and is distinguished by a high cultivation rate of perennial crops. The number of species varies between 12 and 63, on average 40, in the Diepholz-Nienburger area 24 species. 40% of the wild fields contain at least one of the 17 species registered on the Red List of Endangered Species. Populations with many individual plants are the exception and are only found on sites that have been under cultivation for a longer time. The quantitative determination of plant species browsed on by roe deer and brown hares (number of individuals) al well as the assessment of plant species specific browsing preferences (ratio of fields where the species is browsed to the number of fields where the species occurs) resulted in 23 cultivated and 67 wild plant species. Sweet grasses, aromatic and low growing plant species are eaten less than taller juicier plants or plants with milky juice. The rough thistle (Sonchus asper L.) was especially preferred. The richer in species the wild field, the more species are browsed by game. The wild flora of the wild fields thus fulfills an obligatory function in the summer's nutritional spectrum. A cover of over 80% with crop plants results, in contrast, in a decrease in browsing as well as in a tendency towards a reduction in the number of red listed species. Through the regular cultivation of potentially farmable marginal sites the wild fields contribute to the preservation of Segetal vegetation, the restitution of which in the case of small populations is urgently necessary. A pilot project for "compensatory measures for game browsing areas" hat been conceived to fully utilize natural site potentials. DA
