1230.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Soil disturbance - Major force for vegetation dynamics in sand habitats; consequences for management measures in nature conservation
AU  - Jentsch, A.
AU  - Beyschlag, W.
AU  - Nezadal, W.
AU  - Steinlein, T.
AU  - Welß, W.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - This contribution aims at imparting insights from vegetation ecology in dry acidic grasslands for practical nature conservation. In these habitats small-scale disturbances have proved to be of high relevance for the long-term stability of vegetation dynamics. The study was carried out on a former military training area in northern Bavaria. The vegetation consists of a mosaic of plant communities ranging from open pioneer to ruderal stages and calluna heathland. The vegetation pattern cannot entirely be explained as successional chronosequence along a productivity gradient; initial plant assemblages developed either into long-term, stable pioneer communities (Corynephoretum cladonietosum) or to ruderal grassland (Artemisio-Tanacetetum). Experimental soil disturbances were conducted within various successional stages on inland sand dunes. System response was monitored over three subsequent years, thereby testing effects on vegetation, soil seed bank and soil nutrient dynamics. Soil disturbance, such as topsoil turnover, causes a short increase in nitrate (2-3 fold) and ammonia (1.5-2 fold). Three to four years after disturbance species number and cumulative species frequency resemble undisturbed vegetation again. As regards re-establishment, dominant plant life strategies (Frey and Hensen 1995) are either annual shuttle species or species with both sexual and clonal reproduction, which may reach sites by seed dispersal and then gain dominance by vegetative growth. Disturbance impact on nutrient and vegetation dynamics is lowest on sites of low or high soil nitrogen content, and largest at sites of intermediate soil nitrogen content. Most pioneer species in dry acidic grasslands lack primary dormancy and have soil seed banks that are neither large nor long-term persistent. In dry acidic grasslands vegetation recovery after disturbance is barely supported by soil seed banks. The data provide clear evidence that low nutrient availability and frequent small-scale disturbances play a key role for the persistence of biodiversity and the stability of vegetation dynamics in dry acidic grasslands. Appropriate measures for nature conservation are suggested. DA
