TI  - Influence of the substrate on the vegetation development on a flat roof - Eight years of monitoring
AU  - Lenzin, H.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - In 1995 three different substrata were brought out on the flat roof of the Institute NLU ('Natur-, Landschafts- und Umweltschutz') of the University of Basel (Switzerland). They were spread in three different layers of 5, 8 and 12 cm, leading to nine plots. Afterwards 33 plant species were sown or planted. The control in 2003 showed that 23 (70 %) of these species had established. The 38 species which had established spontaneously have come up to 62 % the total species number. Among the spontaneous plants four species (Minuartia hybrida, Myosotis ramossissima, Petrorhagia prolifera and Vulpia myuros) are at least regionally endangered. The six spontaneous neophytes Amaranthus retroflexus, Conyza canadensis, Erigeron annuus ssp. septentrionalis, Galinsoga parviflora, Ailanthus altissima and Sedum spurium only comprise 16 % of the total of spontaneous species, at least 4 % under standard proportion of neophytes in central European cities. The mean floristic similarity of the plots does not depend on the mean spatial distances between them. Decisive factors for a mean floristic similarity and the number of the species are type and thickness of the substrate, determining which species can establish. The high proportion of spontaneous species, the endangered species spontaneously occuring, the small portion of neophytes and the uncertainty concerning origin and purity of the seeds, allow the conclusion that, from a botanical conservation view, it should be considered to apply nutrient-poor and thin-layered substrate (up to 12 cm) on flat roofs, at least in relatively warm and dry climates, as occurring in the Upper Rhine region, and to leave it to self-greening, especially if it is not possible to harvest seeds from similar adjacent habitats. DA
