1241.
TY  - CHAP
TI  - Land use change and soil degradation in a nature protected area of East-Central Europe
AU  - Centeri, C.
AU  - Akác, A.
AU  - Jakab, G.
T2  - Land Use: Planning, Regulations, and Environment
AB  - In recent years, during ecological and land use researches historical maps are used with increasing frequency often to reconstruct the contemporary looks of the landscape. Information about the historical background is especially important at protected natural areas with prominent landscape and natural values. In the course of researches and land use planning for nature conservation purposes - besides the current status - it is worth considering information about former state of an area and the processes which has led to the current status. The GIS (Geographic Information System) provides facilities to superpose different mapping files in digital form and compare their information contents directly. Use of GIS-based data is widespread prevalent for characterization of landscape changes, with applying further landscape indices we can quantify the landscape structure. The major aim of the paper is to provide information on the scale of the landscape change through the analysis of the historical maps in order to prove that the examined area has not undergone dramatic changes and it worth protecting natural values on this area. Historical maps are the fundamental sources of historical information, especially military survey maps from the 18th century. These maps show detailed and consistent information about the contemporary landscape change. The first three Military Survey maps provide information about the state of an area of a particular date, moreover relevant experience and background knowledge allow the compilation of retrospective habitat maps. The first step of the historical research is to trace the historical evolution of the landscape from the first until the current state comparing the first three Military Survey maps. The Landscape Protection Area of Tarna Region is located in the mountainous middle part of the Heves-Borsod-hills in Northern Hungary. It was established in 1993 and covers 9569.8 hectares. The settlements around the landscape protection area suggest that land use and human effects took place early in the history. On the one hand land use change research show the differences in land use, on the other hand it provides information for future land use planning. During the analyses we focus in particular on soil degradation effects on the studied area because huge gullies characterized the area as can be seen on recent topography maps. These gullies are recently under forests and thus they are good indicators of the former misuse of the area. The analyses show the scale of these degradation processes and call attention on its negative environmental affects. Carefully chosen slope sections were examined in the field and based on the data collected on-site and from available data sources, soil erosion modeling were conducted. Soil erosion modeling allows us to provide exact figures for farmers, foresters, for local and regional decision makers in order to prepare a proper land utilization plan to avoid negative effects and to maximize the benefits for the use of natural (forests and grasslands) or seminatural areas. © 2012 by Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved. DA
