1246.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - The contribution of Integrated Pest Management to preservation of endangered wild plant species
AU  - El Titi, A.
T2  - Gesunde Pflanzen
AB  - Ecological infrastructures on farmland are considered as one of the main prerequisites for Integrated Pest Management (IPM). As hedgerows, headlands vegetations or flowering strips at field-margins they provide beneficial arthropods, birds and mamals with food, shelter and overwintering refuges. By this mean gradations of noxious herbivores can be controlled. Long-term investigations at the estate of Lautenbach near Heilbronn (1977-1994) had clearly documented the beneficial effects of such ecological infrastructure on the entomofauna. Comparable studies at another site - Bruchsal - were initiated between 1988-1996 on seven farms of 15 pilot farms for integrated Farming (Arbeitskreis Integrierte Landbewirtschaftung Bruchsal (AKIL)). A complete botanical mapping of 64 plots at Lautenbach and its neighbourhood on one hand and on fields of seven AKIL-farms on the other hand has been conducted to verify, if such ecological infrastructure contributes to re-establishment of endangered or extincted native plant species, in particular species officially mentioned in the Red-List of the State of Baden-Wurttemberg. A regular survey of weed flora on the cropped field sections - being assessed as weed plants or weed seeds in soil seed banks- provided a complementary data rounding up the species diversity. At Lautenbach, 9-13 different plant species were recorded over all studied locations indicating an impoverish spectrum of wild plant species at this site. Lack of disturbance-free off-field-areas has obviously enhanced the development of Couch-grass (Agropyron repens)-dominating rather than the expected Arrhenaterun elatior communities. The occurrence of Nettlepatches (Urtica dioica) showed up the high soil nutrient levels. The appearance of Dactylis glomerata, Ranunculus repens and Potentilla anserina indicated compaction and sub-soil dampy conditions. At rather small spots Brachypodium pinnatum was recorded. Along the water coarse of Lautenbach a sedge-community (Phragmites australis) was established. Typical ruderal species of barren soils were discovered in just a single case, where the plant community was able to survive over rather long periods. In this case the field margin vegetation was rather short and of a higher species diversity. Due to the prevailance of nutrient rich soils at Lautenbach, indicator species of barren soils were present just as exceptions. However, these 'exceptions' hosted a Red-List-species of this region, namely (Coronopus squamatus). Within the very densely planted 17 species of trees and shrubs of the Lautenbach-hedgerows - known to be of the same age structure - only single species succeeded to move into the hedgerow-habitat. The prevailing species were Cornus sanguinea, Ligustrum vulgare, Rosa canina and Corylus avellana. The studies showed that the species composition of the field margin flora in the surroundings was rather similiar to that of Lautenbach or to that of boarder-sections of meagre soils and/or thermophile plant communities. On the contrary to Lautenbach the community surveys at the Bruchsal-site resulted in a number of wild plant species of the Red-List with different endangerment categories. On average two species of A3 and three of A5 were proved to occur at each of the seven study farms. Based on these findings a package of measures can be concluded to insure the highest diversity on farmland. These include: 1. Arrangement and structuring of agricultural landscapes by lineal plantations of hedgerows, field margins, single trees, shrubs and small grouped shrubs are not only feasible but also practical. 2. Existing hedgerows should be cut-back every 8-15 years period. 3. Hedgerow new planting should provide at least 6 m width, inequal distributed indigenous and site-typical species and should maintain 3-4 m interface margins at both hedgerow sides. The interface-vegetation is to be mowed at 1 m height. 4. Field margins should have at least 5 m width, at water coarses 10 m, to be left unsown for self-establishment and on the long-term run, to be mowed every second or third year. 5. Flowering field margin-strips should be drilled at low-seed rates of at least 3 m width. 6. Existing ruderal flora should be regularly mowed and the cuts should be transported away. Driving on field margins should be minimized. 7. Set-aside and annual rotational fallow as well as the regenerative establishment of plant communities without sowing on chalky-top field locations (pararendzina) is likely to contribute to species enrichment. DA
