1306.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Methods for creating the environmental indicator 'population development of selected birds'- How to aggregate faunistic data to an integrative assessment?
AU  - Schlumprecht, H.
AU  - Schupp, D.
AU  - Südbeck, P.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - Main purpose of environmental indicators is to provide generally intelligible information about state and trends of the environment. In the field of biodiversity, the development of populations of selected species is regarded as an important indicator, however holding the problem to quantify them and relate them to biodiversity targets. During a test phase for the set of environmental indicators for Lower Saxony an indicator was defined describing the population development of selected bird species based on an index in relation to future targets. First, the paper discusses advantages of target-based indicators and then describes the method to establish two indicators, one response-indicator and one state-indicator, by means of standardised experts consultation, using the socalled Delphi Technique. The choice of representative species, the definition of interim targets (by the year 2010) for each species, the calculation of indices related to the target, and the aggregation of the species-indices to the state-and the response-indicator are explained. The paper finally presents the results of these two indicators and of selected ecological groups of the state-indicator (bird species of the main biotope types grassland/meadows, forests, arable fields, settlements). In contrast to hither-to existing biodiversity indicators, the setting of targets for a certain period of time enables quality management and controlling of environmental policy and activities. This practical approach leads to an improved consideration of nature conservation in both public awareness of biodiversity and political debate. DA
