1375.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Grasshoppers on mountain grassland - Role of land-use intensity and climate, effects for practical nature conservation
AU  - Kampmann, D.
AU  - Herzog, F.
AU  - Konold, W.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - Permanent grasslands dominate the agricultural landscape of the Swiss Alps. Increasing intensification of agricultural practices has lead to a declining species diversity, affecting both flora and fauna. Studies on the Swiss Plateau have shown positive effects of reduced land-use intensity on the species diversity of grasshoppers induced by the Swiss Agri-environmental Programme. On the mountainous regions however the results are not consistent. Goal of the study was to quantify the impact of three habitat characteristics (intensity of land use, exposition, surrounding habitat structures) on individual grasshopper species and on their species diversity in the Swiss Alps. The results indicate that below 800 m land use intensity has a major impact on the diversity of grasshopper species, whilst between 800 m and 1400 m no signifcant effect was found. At altitudes above 1400m south facing slopes with low land-use intensity showed the highest species richness, probably due to the fact that here species from lower elevations still occur owing to the warmer microclimate. These findings allow the conclusion that in regions below 800 m the species diversity of grasshoppers is a suitable indicator for the degree of land use intensity. Beyond 800 m the analysis should either focus on single indicator species (800 - 1400m) or has to additionally consider the exposition of the study area (> 1400 m). The situating of ecological compensation sites in regions below 800 m might enhance species richness, between 800 and 1400 m it rather promotes individual species. At very high altitudes (> 1400 m) the reduction of land use intensity would especially support species diversity on south facing slopes. Several species of the Red Data Book, however, neither prefer low management intensity nor southern exposition. Conservation efforts at these altitudes should pay respect to habitat requirements of individual endangered grasshopper species rather than targeting species diversity as a whole. DA
