1380.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Ecosystem services assessment of German baltic lagoons
AU  - Inácio, M.
AU  - Schernewski, G.
AU  - Nazemtseva, Y.
AU  - Paysen, P.
AU  - Friedland, R.
T2  - 13th International MEDCOAST Congress on Coastal and Marine Sciences, Engineering, Management and Conservation, MEDCOAST 2017
AB  - Coastal zones are among the most productive habitats around the globe. Viewed as important areas in a biological and anthropogenic perspective, coastal zones host nearly half of the global population. This is due to the environment.s ability to provide ecosystem goods and services that are much enjoyed by humans. Ecosystem services can be defined as all contributions of the environment to human well-being, and are divided into Provisioning, Regulating & Maintenance and Cultural Services categories The popularity ecosystem services as a research topic have been increasing in the past decade, hence, the main focus has been the assessment and quantification of services in the terrestrial realm creating a discrepancy regarding knowledge and research when comparing to the marine realm. More recently, the concept is being used as a tool to assess the value of nature and it has been adapted to be used as a support tool for management approaches like Integrated Coastal Management and Maritime Spatial Planning. In Europe, target 2 of Biodiversity 2020 Strategy and the Marine Strategy Framework Directive advises the Member States to join efforts for restoring and maintaining the ecosystems and their services. Within this framework, we developed a tool to assess ecosystem services in the marine environment, the Ecosystem Services Assessment Tool. The approach of the tool assesses services for two time periods representing different ecologic status of a water body. In total, 31 ecosystem services represented by 54 indicators are assessed for the two periods, and classify the change in indicators using a scale from-5 to 5 representing an increase or decrease in services provision over time. The tool was applied to different water bodies in the German Baltic coast: Szczecin Lagoon, Darss Zingst and Schlei. The objective is to compare the different systems regarding the number of services provided for each location. Also, we want to compare if the changes in services provision differ from each study area. From the preliminary results we observed, in general, a decrease in Provisioning and Regulating & Maintenance sections and an increase in Cultural section. Analysing the changes in services provision from the past to present is important to understand the dynamics of ecosystem services, furthermore, this information can be into consideration from the Member States in their future efforts to restore and maintain ecosystem services. DA
