1390.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - The role of networks and policy entrepreneurs in the establishment of the Swabian Alb Biosphere Reserve
AU  - Gerner, J.
AU  - Schraml, U.
T2  - Allgemeine Forst- und Jagdzeitung
AB  - The successful establishment of large scale protected areas is quite a challenge for nature conservation policies in practice. The implementation of protected areas and management measures are frequently accompanied by conflicts between involved actors (e.g. RAO et al., 2003; MÉNDEZ-CONTRERAS et al., 2008; GERNER et al., 2011). The present article analyses the establishment of the Biosphere Reserve (BR) Swabian Alb, an example for successful conflict management and a high level of acceptance in its surroundings (SCHIEBER, 2009). It is located about 50 km south-east of the capital of Baden-Wuerttemberg (South-West Germany), one of 16 "Laender" that constitute Germany. It was established in 2008 (an administrative act at state level, establishment through UNESCO in 2009) and covers an area of 850 km2, 26 km2of which constitutes the "core area" (UNESCO, 2012). It is characterized by its beech forests on steep hillsides and canyons ("Albtrauf"), its widespread traditionally extensive sparse orchards ("Streuobstwiesen"), and manifold agricultural uses, such as the production of wine and crop farming. 29 communities out of three different administrative districts are part of the BR (see Figure 1). By analyzing the case study we hope to identify which factors crucially contributed to its establishment and positive reception. The analysis is based on twelve semi-structured interviews that were conducted with responsible staff from different administrations, affected communities, and representatives of non-administrative organizations (see Table 1). The interviews were structured by breaking down the establishment-process into two phases: formation of the political idea to create a protected area, and the following implementation process. The first phase was analyzed using the theoretical model of the Multiple Streams Framework, the second phase by network-theoretical considerations. The seed for the Swabian Alb BR was planted when the decision was made to abandon a large military training area in the region. The forthcoming departure of the forces prompted questions about the future management of the area. Large parts of it were considerably contaminated by ammunitions. The special use of the area had created a unique landscape and special biodiversity. A solution for the future use of the area was proposed by the owner (Administration of the Federal Republic of Germany) to politicians on state and regional level: specifically the establishment of a large scale protected area. This suggestion was welcomed by all affected political levels as well as the neighboring communities of the military training area. Together, the establishment of a BR was decided to be most suitable for the region and its conditions. Once this decision was made, the implementation process began. The interviewees named several factors that they thought were crucial for the successful establishment of the BR: the support on all political levels, the integrative way in which the implementation process was conducted (for example, all actors took part in formulating the administrative act) and not least the type of protected area that was to be established. BRs explicitly not only aim at the protection of nature but also at regional development and the sustainable use of natural resources. Thus, except for in the core areas of BRs, human activities still take place. Most of the actors felt the establishment of the BR to be a win-win-situation. That resulted in a considerable engagement of the regional actors in the implementation process. Furthermore, the natural conditions in the region foster an agricultural use of the landscape in line with the goals of a BR. Apart from this, the actors themselves, their attitudes, commitment, and pragmatism as well as the networks between the actors were identified as being the most important factors, creating constant communication structures and trust which enabled the actors to discuss the possible establishment of the BR at a factual level and to openly address critical issues. The case study of the Swabian Alb BR illustrates the importance of continued links between actors when establishing large scale protected areas. These networks helped responsible administrations to recognize changing local moods so that they were able to adapt to changing situations and needs. The integration of involved actors was crucial for their joint identification with goals and aims of the protected area and thus for the successful establishment of the BR. © 2014, J.D. Sauerlaender's Verlag. All rights reserved. DA
