AU  - Hanewald, K.
T2  - Gefahrstoffe Reinhaltung der Luft
AB  - In the year 2005, lichen mappings were carried out in the cities of Wetzlar and Giessen (Hesse, Germany) to assess the degree of air pollution. The towns had already been examined for their lichen cover in 1970, 1985 and 1995. The results obtained in and after 1985 could be compared directly with each other in accordance with the new lichen mapping guideline VDI 3957 part 13; earlier results could only be compared in parts. In 1970, 1985 and 2005 comparative investigations of the pH values were carried out on the tree barks which had been used for lichen mapping. The results can be summarised as follows: - In both cities, the bark pH values have been rising due to reduced sulphur dioxide concentration since the beginning of the 1980s, and meanwhile, the values have almost reached pre-industrial levels. - During the first examination in 1970, complete impoverishment of the lichen flora was recorded due to extremely high air pollution; several lichen-free areas were observed in the industrialised northern part of Wetzlar. - The number of recolonisers rose in line with increasing improvement in air pollution within the investigation period; in addition, more sensitive species could be found to an increasing extent. - Between 1970 and 2005, there was a considerable increase in species diversity; this was in part due to eutrophicating air pollutants. DA
