TI  - Grazing management in sand ecosystems - Research concept and first results in the Northern Upper Rhine Valley
AU  - Zehm, A.
AU  - Storm, C.
AU  - Nobis, M.
AU  - Gebhardt, S.
AU  - Schwabe, A.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - Sand ecosystems (pioneer vegetation, grassland, pine forest and ecotones) belong to the vanishing and endangered biotope types in Germany, holding numerous species from the Red Data Book. Succession, ruderalisation and dominance of grass or single species are important contributing factors. From a nature conservation point of view it is therefore necessary to develop concepts for the protection and restitution of these biotope types, which guarantee the necessary dynamics and are economically feasible. Grazing with extensive breeds (e.g. sheep) could be such a concept. The paper presents the research project "Inland Sand Ecosystems: Dynamics and Restitution" of the BMBF (Federal Ministry for Education and Research), which investigates the effects of grazing on sand ecosystems. It is carried out by the Universities of Darmstadt, Osnabrück and Lüneburg together with an expert office in co-operation with the University of Göttingen. The paper describes investigation areas, research concepts, sampling design and grazing systems. The project consists of five modules: aspects of vegetation structure, biocoenotic connections with animals, nutrient dynamics, dynamics of arthropod populations and socio-economy. The data are analysed, summarised and scaled up in a Geographic Information System (GIS). First results show that sheep e.g. prefer nutrient-rich, especially ruderal species, and generally neglect many of the typical sand-specific endangered species. In pine forests mono-dominant stands of Calamagrostis epigejos and Rubus fruticosus agg. are intensively grazed, leading to increasing biodiversity and higher density of pine seedlings. After complete data analysis recommendations for nature conservation measures will be developed applicable to other sand ecosystems. DA
