TI  - Restoration of former coal-mining landscapes in east Germany - endangered secondary biotopes: occurrence of highly endangered animal species in Goitsche (Bitterfeld) open-cast mine
AU  - Geissler-Strobel, S.
AU  - Bugner, J.
AU  - Feldmann, R.
AU  - Gunther, K.
AU  - Gras, J.
AU  - Herbst, F.
AU  - Seluga, K.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - After the German re-unification in 1990, 44 open cast coal-mines were abandoned in East-Germany. Today only 50% (400 km2) of the areas affected by mining activities have been recultivated. The open cast mines in East Germany represent last refuges for many endangered animal species. These species will disappear if future recultivation does not consider the demands of nature protection. A research project, funded by the Deutsche Bundesstiftung Umwelt, studies the abundance and species composition of different animal groups in these areas. The study site is Goitsche (near to the city of Bitterfeld), one of the largest open cast mines in East-Germany, which is to be recultivated within the next few years. To protect at least parts of the present populations, the recultivation management has to take into account the habitat requirements of endangered species that have already established. On the basis of habitat and animal species mapping in Goitsche, the study considers economically as well as ecologically effective forms of recultivation. From the initial results fundamental requirements can be derived for sensitive landscape restoration respecting the conservation of valuable habitats. Future monitoring is necessary to review the concept. DA
