1613.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Course and valuation of agricultural landscape changes in East Germany since 1945
AU  - Philipp, HJ
T2  - BERICHTE UBER LANDWIRTSCHAFT
AB  - In chapter 1 six phases and numerous elements of the extremely diverse and rapid changes experienced by the East German agricultural landscapes since 1945, are outlined, based on an extensive literary analysts. Changes of the landscape structure and picture occurred mainly in the land reform phase in the second half of the 40's, and in the agricultural cooperation and agricultural industrialisation phases between the mid-60's and the beginning of the 80's. Much less change occurred during the collectivisation, the correction, and up to present in the transformation phases of the farming sector, thus in the 50's, 80's, and 90's, respectively. Chapter 2 describes the research questions and techniques as well as the two northeastern Brandenburg community districts and 72 of their adult inhabitants selected by the author for a landscape-oriented survey. Randomly selected residents of purposely selected small communities were asked to judge the following: 1) landscape changes they experienced since their youth or local settlement, such as local large-scale farm buildings, set aside fields, and environmental pollution, 2) which landscape changes they prefer in the future, and 3) if they were ready to contribute to the realisation of their preferences by either voluntary gift or work or both. The results from this survey are presented and analysed by either voluntary gift or work or lysed within the last chapter.; The phases differentiated as well as elements treated reflect the tremendous impact of an extraordinarily unstable agricultural policy within only 50 years. This confirms the Viennese geographer HUGO HASSINGER who in the beginning of the 30's discovered that the governmental-political sphere is a powerful, although only indirect landscape architect. The many far-reaching and major agricultural landscape changes at the times of the Soviet Occupation Zone and the German Democratic Republic show the demands of the political system, more precisely, the uniformity, technocratic voluntarism, and also gigantism of a centralistic economic order and territorial planning that was at the expense of ecological balance and scenic attraction. Since the German unification, the landscape impact ''from the outside'', caused by regulations and other interventions of the EC, federal, regional, and local government politics, have decreased only a little. The partly sensitive registration and the predominantly negative valuation of the landscape changes in the physical environs of the 72 rural people interviewed reflect an excess of such interventions. It is recommended that an extended Investigation of the recent agricultural landscape changes in East Germany (as well as West Germany) be undertaken, based on contemporary witnesses, local chronicles, available records, and other sources.; DA
