1809.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - The Crested Lark Galerida cristata in Switzerland - Area expansion and disappearance in the 20th century
AU  - Hegelbach, J.
AU  - Güntert, M.
AU  - Winkler, R.
T2  - Ornithologische Beobachter
AB  - In central Europe the Crested Lark has been known since the middle ages as witnessed by references in works by Albertus Magnus (1200-1280). Claretus (1360) and Hans Sachs (1494-1576). The abundance and the range of this species expanded considerably in the 19th century. Switzerland was affected by these changes in the second half of the 19th century with the breeding population reaching its maximum size between 1890 and 1930. The colonization must have occurred through the Lowlands of the Upper Rhine (Alsace, Breisgau) and was restricted to low lying areas in the northwestern half of the country. The Swiss journal «Der Ornithologische Beobachter» was founded in 1902 during this period of maximum abundance. The first breeding record in Switzerland came from Basle in 1859 or shortly thereafter. In the following years up to approximately 1910, the cities of Berne, Thun, Aarau, Brugg, and Olten were colonized including other villages in this area. Substantial breeding populations could be found in the city of Zurich and in its suburbs along the river Limmat, and in and around Winterthur and Schaffhausen. The area around Basle was an important bridge-head during the expansion and the retreat of the species and served as the connection to the populations in the Lowlands of the Upper Rhine. Around 1900 the Crested Lark was breeding not only in Basle but also in the surrounding villages. Most breeding populations in Switzerland went extinct shortly after 1920. Crested Larks lasted the longest in Berne (perhaps until 1940), in Aarberg (certain records until 1934) and in Solothurn (confirmed until 1937). Crested Larks were still breeding in Schaffhausen in 1925, and in and around Zurich in 1937. The species lasted by far the longest in Basle and its surroundings where the last successful brood was recorded in 1976 and the last breeding attempt in 1983. The last breeding attempt near the border on the French side occurred in 1991. The canton Ticino was never inhabited, the city of Geneva probably in 1924 and 1925, and the city of Yverdon in 1938 and 1945. A single brood was recorded in Chur in 1954. The expansion of the Crested Lark was not correlated with climatic changes after 1850. The average yearly temperature declined slightly between 1850 and 1900 which would argue against the expansion of this warmth-seeking species during that period. The decline of the species also did not coincide with a general decrease in temperature. What is certain is that the disappearance was intensified by the decline in suitable habitat. These negative trend currently continues in the northeastern parts of the species range. DA
