1821.
TY  - CHAP
TI  - Zoobenthos diversity and recent changes in the major European rivers: The Volga and the Danube
AU  - Schletterer, M.
AU  - Humpesch, U.H.
AU  - Graf, W.
T2  - River Channels: Types, Dynamics and Changes
AB  - The Volga, with a length of 3531 km and a basin area of 1,431 Million km2, is the largest river in Europe and the most important waterway in Russia. The Volga basin is the nucleus of the Russian civilisation and comprises nowadays 40 % of the population, 45 % of the country's industry and 50 % of its agriculture. Especially the 20th century brought a significant change for the Volga River: a cascade of hydroelectric power plants was constructed along its course and therefore most of the hydrobiological studies in the Volga basin were focusing on lentic water bodies, i.e. reservoirs. However, recently the free-flowing section in the headwaters between the Upper Volga Lakes and the city of Tver was identified as a refuge area for the potamal fauna of European importance.The Danube is about 2850 km long and thus it is the second largest river in Europe. It arises in the Black Forest in Germany and discharges into the Black Sea. According to the International Commission for the Protection of the Danube Basin (ICPDR) the catchment with a total area of 801,463 km2 includes the territories of 19 countries and is the home of 83 million people with a wide range of cultures, languages and historical backgrounds. The ecosystems of the Danube River Basin are highly valuable in environmental, economic, historical and social terms, but they are subject to increasing pressure and serious pollution from agriculture, industry and cities. The above mentioned numbers illustrate and emphasise the enormous international importance of this river.In this chapter the main interest focuses on the investigation of biodiversity of macroinvertebrates of the free-flowing sections, i.e. the number of species and their relative proportions in the whole invertebrate community, as well as major governing environmental factors. A study of biodiversity is of fundamental importance within the field of ecology, in order to address questions such as why some species are numerous and others are rare, and what kind of influence their relative abundances have on the structure and function of the whole community. Furthermore a review on research history, longitudinal specifics, faunistical changes and invasion pathways as well as management guidelines will be presented. © 2012 Nova Science Publishers, Inc. All rights reserved. DA
