TY  - JOUR
TI  - Impacts of flooding and forest management on biodiversity of molluscs and xylobiontic beetles in Bavarian floodplain forests
AU  - Strätz, C.
AU  - Schmidl, J.
AU  - Bail, J.
AU  - Müller, J.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - Along a section of the River Danube between the towns of 'Dillingen' and 'Ingolstadt' (Bavaria) a comparative study was conducted comparing forest reserves and managed forests within and behind the flooded area. The results revealed that floodplain forests with close-to-nature features show a striking biodiversity of snails and xylobiontic beetles. The parameters 'flooding' and 'old-growth' can be evaluated as the main factors effecting species richness and abundance of specialized species. The faunal richness covers the whole stand area of old-growth soft- and hardwood floodplain forests with natural flooding and is additionally enriched by special habitats such as "Brennen" (gravel areas above flood level), mudplains and reed. A decline in faunistic and nature conservation value has been recorded towards increasing forest management and suppressed flooding dynamics. From a zoological point of view the most valuable stands are hardwood forests with oak and ash and softwood forests with willow and autochthonous poplar, both within the natural flooded area. Molluscs largely benefit from deadwood and are supported by natural flooding dynamics. The insights gained from the mollusc fauna match with those from xylobiontic beetles. In the close-to-nature floodplains forests 138 species of xylobiontic beetles were recorded, which is approx. 10% of all species known in Bavaria. 35 species are listed in the Red Data Book of Bavaria (category '0' to '3'), three more species are listed as 'G', with a 25% proportion of endangered species. The distribution of the beetle species correlates with the tree species and with the supply of deadwood and old-growth structures respectively. In the dry, outbanked stands a depauverated diversity was identified with lower species abundances and smaller proportions of endangered species of xylobiontic beetles. DA
