1957.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Development of spatial structure and diversity in strict forest reserves in Northwest-Germany
AU  - Meyer, P.
AU  - Pogoda, P.
T2  - Allgemeine Forst- und Jagdzeitung
AB  - The paper describes the development of the spatial structure and diversity in near-natural unmanaged beech- and beech-oak forest reserves, based on data collected on three consecutive occasions during the past 25 years. The structure indices (mingling and diameter differentiation) are combined with the SHANNON-Index. This method can be used for analyzing the relationship of the neighbourhood constellations and the change of spatial structure over time. The results reveal that the structural diversity in most of the examined beech forests is less pronounced than in the oak forests. Natural regeneration is prominent in canopy gaps and occurs in scattered patches. This leads to an increase of structural diversity in most of the observed forest stands. Selective removal of single trees or groups of trees mimics natural processes. The specific type of spatial structure is related to the growing conditions of the site, to competition and disturbances. The possible options for near-natural forest management are varied and there is no unanimity about optimum silviculture. DA
