AB  - For 72 north-west German districts and towns the abundance (hunting bags) of the;brown hare from 1989/1990 to 1996/1497 was calculated for a 1000 ha hunting area(Fig. 1 and 2), and together with 7 variables of land use and soil quality correlated per annum in 8 factor analysis (Tab. 1 to 8). In each analysis 4 features (factors) of characteristic landscape structure were pointed our: Factor 1 = agrarian lowlands - In the 8-year period the grain and maize fields, with their positive valuations, have an increasing influence on the brown hare population density; whereas root crop areas (beet and potatoes) show a decreasing effect on the animals. Factor 2 = urban areas - The high loadings of traffic areas with reducing valuations were of prime importance for the determination of this feature. Factor 3 = coastal region - This landscape, situated on the German North Sea coast, shows the very high marked values of pasture land with decreasing influence on hares. Factor 4 = low mountain range The positive woodland valuations, with decreasing influence on the brown hare, are responsible for the determination of this factor. For all 4 regions we found decreasing loadings of the animals. Soil quality shows in the agrarian lowlands, where the hares are mostly bagged, a notable drop in advantageous conditions, but an increase of conditions condusive to population growth in the other 3 regions. DA
