2057.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - On the beach ... in London
AU  - O'Halloran, K.
T2  - Water
AB  - Slow sand filters have a central role in the history of water treatment, particularly in London, with James Simpson applying the first large-scale filter for the Chelsea Water Company in London in 1829. Later, as the benefits of such a system became apparent, the Metropolis Water Act of 1852 required that all Thames-derived water in an area of five miles around St Paul's Cathedral be slow sand filtered. Worldwide acceptance of the value of slow sand filters came in 1892. The neighbouring cities of Hamburg and Altona both abstracted drinking water from the Elbe, but for Altona the water was slow sand filtered and for Hamburg the treatment consisted of settling only. When the raw water was infected by encamped emigrants a massive cholera outbreak ensued. Whereas Hamburg, a city of 580,000 inhabitants, had 19,891 infections with 7,582 deaths, Altona with 143,000 inhabitants had only 578 infections with 328 deaths. Although London continues with slow sand filtration it is difficult to perceive a rapid growth in this technology for other urban areas as the extent of land required is considerable. As an illustration, Walton, with a reliable output of 130ML/day, requires around 26,500m2 of sand as well as the associated land in and around the filters for cleaning equipment. Whilst not practicable for urban areas, these filters offer a viable alternative for remote communities where the requirements for land poses no real restriction. DA
