2160.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Silvicultural management options to preserve the seepage-water quality under forest - case study conventwald
AU  - Puhlmann, H.
AU  - Von Wilpert, K.
T2  - Hydrologie und Wasserbewirtschaftung
AB  - The Conventwald ecosystem study is an ongoing research project that started in 1991 and comprises now more than 15 years of uninterrupted observations of the water and element cycles in a forested area including a preserved natural mixed forest and several silviculturally managed stands. Examining a nutrient-rich and climatically favourable region in the Southern Black Forest, the field study aims mainly at assessing the deterioration of the formerly excellent nutrient status - and consequently, the risk of debasing tree growth - caused by atmospheric depositions from anthropogenic sources. In the study area, various silvicultural management options, e.g. tree type/age composition and harvesting methods, have been tested and evaluated with respect to their ability to counteract a probable soil chemical drift. The study demonstrated that water- and element fluxes depend very closely on tree species composition and on the small-scale variation of the crown density. The time behaviour of the runoff from catchments covered with spatially heterogeneous mixed species stands can be understood only through an assessment of the (area-weighted) contribution of the different stand situations (tree species, age stages, gaps, etc.) in the catchment. The comparison of the different stand situations in this study showed that stands with dominating beech caused decisively lower nitrate and sulphate leakages from the root zone than spruce mono-cultured stands. Accordingly, beech stands showed a generally lower flux density below the rooting zone than spruce stands. The interruption of the crown layer through harvesting operations resulted in heavily increased nitrate fluxes-this applied for a clear-cut area, as well as for small gaps created by cutting of a few mature trees. DA
