TY  - JOUR
TI  - Renaturation of a landfill site by allowing spontaneous succession and directed succession - Results of 10 years of monitoring
AU  - Rebele, F.
AU  - Lehmann, C.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - On a landfill site in the Northeast of Berlin trial plots for permanent monitoring were established in 1996. The landfill mainly contains building rubble and has been covered with sandy, slightly alkaline subsoil of low fertility. The study investigated changes in species composition and cover through ten years of vegetation development. Five plots were left unmanaged, five plots were mown twice per year, and five plots were mown every five years. The nutrient-poor raw soil was colonized very quickly by species of all life forms, with woody perennials (especially willow and poplar) germinating from the beginning. Vegetation was dominated by the tall rhizomatous grass Calamagrostis epigejos from the third year onwards, except on the plots mown twice per year. Spontaneous succession runs very quickly towards woodland, forming a shrub layer (> 1 m) from the third year and a tree layer (> 5m) from the ninth year. Mowing after five years of vegetation development had no significant effect on the dominance of Calamagrostis epigejos. Also, woody plants were not significantly affected by mowing every five years, due to rapid vegetative regeneration of willow and poplar; scrub encroachment was even enhanced by this management type. Mowing twice per year was very effective for controlling Calamagrostis epigejos and keeping habitats open. Vegetation on permanently mown plots developed towards dry grassland. Mean species diversity significantly increased from 20 species per 4 m2 at the start towards 30 species per 4 m2 during ten years of vegetation development. DA
