TI  - Beech forests in Germany - Implementation of a protection strategy
AU  - Panek, N.
T2  - Naturschutz und Landschaftsplanung
AB  - Natural or semi-natural stands of Common Beech Forests have become some of the most endangered forest types in Europe. In 2007 Carpathian primeval forests were acknowledged by the UNESCO as World Natural Heritage. Particularly in Germany, however, only very view remnants have been protected; the original area shrank by more than 90% due to human influence. From a global point of view Germany has a particular responsibility for the preservation of beech forests. Already in 1992 the Convention of Biodiversity defined basic strategic approaches for their protection; Articles 6a and 8 of the Convention provide the basis for policy recommendations of a 'National Strategy for Biodiversity'. Referring to the protection of beech forests the Convention has formulated overall concepts and objectives as well as recommendations for the spatial-conceptual implementation. A spatial protection approach should include both "integrative" as well as "segregative" elements, with the "segregative" part comprising about 10 to 15% of the present beech forest stands for a long-term protection of the biological diversity. Finally, the study details some steps to implement a protection strategy for beech forests and evaluates their feasibility. DA
