TY  - JOUR
TI  - Successful management of Calamagrostis epigejos (L.) Roth on a sandy landfill site
AU  - Lehmann, C.
AU  - Rebele, F.
T2  - Journal of Applied Botany
AB  - We conducted a study on a landfill site in the Northwest of Berlin, Germany, where silty sand of low fertility was colonised by Calamagrostis epigejos (L.) Roth and other grassland and ruderal species. Five plots of 3m × 3m size were mown twice yearly (end of May/beginning of June and end of August) and five plots were left unmown. Species composition and cover of the central 4m2 of each plot were followed over five years of vegetation development with records in spring and late summer. In the fifth year we also determined number of shoots of C. epigejos and above ground dry mass of C. epigejos, other herbaceous species, and woody plants. C. epigejos colonised all plots within one year after the deposition of the substrate. On unmown plots, mean cover of C. epigejos increased towards 35% in the fifth year. Mowing significantly affected C. epigejos, showing mean cover values within a range from < 1% to 6.7%. On unmown plots, mean total cover increased from approx. 10% in the first year to 80% in the fifth year after the start of the succession. On mown plots, total cover also increased to 60% until May of year four, but then decreased towards about 45%. Species richness on unmown plots did not significantly change during the five subsequent years, but there are significant differences between seasons, with higher species numbers in spring. Mowing significantly increased species richness during the course of vegetation development. In the fifth year, species richness on mown plots was 30.4 ± 4.5 species/4m2 (spring) respectively 26.2 ± 3.4 species/4m2 (summer) and 24.6 ± 5.0 species/ 4m2 (spring), respectively 20.6 ± 3.2 species/4m2 (summer) on unmown plots. While mowing only slightly reduced above ground dry mass of other herbaceous species, the effect on C. epigejos was evident. In the fifth year shoot density of C. epigejos on unmown plots was 924 ± 315 shoots/4m2 and 408 ± 255 shoots/4m2 (spring), respectively 347 ± 234 shoots/4m2 (summer), on mown plots. Mean above ground dry mass of C. epigejos on unmown plots was 611.6 ± 255.3 g/4m2 and 56.4 ± 33 g/4m2 (spring) + 28.8 ± 19.6 g/4m2 (summer) = 75.2 ± 50.3 g/4m2 (total) on mown plots. Total above ground dry mass on unmown plots amounted 1676 ± 465.4 g/4m2 and 523.2 ± 76.1 g/4m2 on mown plots. DA
