AU  - Lüthy, P.
T2  - Microbial Control of Insect and Mite Pests: From Theory to Practice
AB  - Mosquito control programs in Europe are predominantly based on products containing the larvicidal toxins of Bacillus thuringiensis subsp. israelensis (Bti) and/or Lysinibacillus sphaericus. Interventions against floodwater mosquitoes in Sweden, Switzerland, Germany, and some parts of metropolitan France solely rely on microbial control agents. Following their discovery, Bti and L. sphaericus were rapidly developed with the support of industry and universities as well as national and international organizations such as the World Health Organization (WHO). The WHO Pesticide Evaluation Scheme (WHOPES), promoting and coordinating the testing and evaluation of pesticides for public health, has evaluated microbial control agents based on the abovementioned two microorganisms as safe and efficient (WHO/CDS/WHOPES/2004.8). The rapid exploitation was facilitated by a series of useful properties. In addition to the high degree of biosafety, the two microorganisms are suitable for mass production. The products are easy to handle, possess a satisfactory shelf life, are cost-effective, and suitable for integrated mosquito management programs, even with community participation. The microbial control agents do not interfere with the biodiversity of aquatic ecosystems. The large variety of formulations for aerial and ground application such as granules, water dispersible powders, and fluid concentrates as well as ice granules or tablets provide the basis for tailor-made mosquito control. The application of microbial control agents can effectively reduce the population densities of the target species such as the floodwater mosquitoes Aedes vexans and Ochlerotatus sticticus and the container breeders Culex pipiens or Aedes albopictus to an acceptable level. Thus the quality of life for humans can be maintained, minimizing the need for personal protection by repellents, screens, and bed nets. © 2017 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved. DA
