2405.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Historical and actual fir growth in Europe - A dendroecological analysis
AU  - Tegel, W.
AU  - Büntgen, U.
T2  - Allgemeine Forst- und Jagdzeitung
AB  - Forest decline played a pivotal role in motivating Europe's political focus on sustainability ∼35 years ago. Silver fir exhibited particularly severe dieback in the mid 1970s, but disentangling biotic from abiotic drivers remained challenging because spatial and temporal data were lacking (e.g. SCHÜTT and COWLING, 1985; INNES, 1987; KANDLER and INNES, 1995). In this study, we analyzed 14.136 samples from living trees and historical timbers (Table 1, Figure 1 and 2). Comparisons of the regional growth curves show a surprisingly high degree of similarities (Fig. 3 and 4). The annual, decadal to multi- centennial fluctuations in radial growth of European fir can be correlated highly significant (Fig. 4 and 5). Even though a dominant climatic driver of European fir growth was not found (Fig. 6), since comparisons of modern fir growth with instrumental climate data are hampered by more emission caused growth control the industrialization (Fig. 5). Ring width extremes were evidently triggered by anomalous variations in Central European April-June precipitation. Wet conditions were associated with dynamic low-pressure cells, whereas continental-scale droughts coincided with persistent high-pressure (BÜNTGEN et al., 2011b). With 356 pollen records we evaluate recent fir growth from a continent-wide and Holocene-long perspective (BÜNTGEN et al., 2014). Land use and climate change influenced forest growth over the last millennium, whereas anthropogenic emissions became important after ∼1850. Pollution control since the 1980s, together with a warmer but not drier climate facilitated an unprecedented surge in fir productivity across Central Europe. Restricted fir distribution before the Mesolithic and again in Modern Times, separated by a Bronze Age zenith in abundance (Fig. 7), is indicative of long term interactions of changing temperatures, shifts in precipitation regimes, and human impacts that have shaped forest structure and productivity. This study suggests that fir growth, in contrast to other species, will likely benefit from a projected warmer but not drier climate in mesic sites, whereas more southern habitats near the species' Mediterranean distribution limit are already showing drought-induced growth depression, which will become even more critical in a drier future. © J.D. Sauerländer's Verlag, Bad Orb, 2015. DA
