2476.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Changes in ecological value of the Danube floodplain forest in last five decades
AU  - Oszlanyi, J
T2  - EKOLOGIA-BRATISLAVA
AB  - The floodplain forests in the alluvium of the Danube river are often said to be the last natural ecosystem in this part of Central Europe. The actual situation had been quite different, even at different times before the Gabcikovo power station was finished. The whole ecosystem of the Danube inland-delta was influenced very intensively by building dikes against floods already in the last century. The floods, originally covering large areas with shallow water were limited by the dikes and thus the floods became more often, higher and more intensive in this restricted area. Luxurious growing conditions were used up by foresters introducing intensive forest management and changing the wood species composition of the forests. The ecological value of forests changed especially due to the planting of poplar clones with extremely high production of wood. Intensive management of poplar monocultures influences also the composition and ecological value of the shrub layer, which is an important part of the forest ecosystems here and plays an important role in their stability. In the present paper the changes in forest ecosystems since 1945 are commented and discussed, based on older data from technical literature and on direct experience in the recent years.; DA
