2497.
TY  - JOUR
TI  - Effects of soil contamination on the animal community of forest ecosystem.
AU  - Roth, M.
AU  - Funke, W.
T2  - Forstwissenschaftliches Centralblatt
AB  - The effects of anthropogenic substances on the soil fauna (Protozoa, Invertebrata) have been investigated in an 85-near-old spruce stand at the southeastern edge of the "Swabian Alb" (South Germany). Of special interest have been the influence of lime, mineral fertilizer, acid solutions, pesticides, and volatile chlorocarbons. Subject of another study has been the potential of metals to accumulate in food webs of soil invertebrates. The application of lime, fertilizer, acid solutions, pesticides, and chlorocarbons caused extreme alterations of the faunal community of the spruce stand. Soil animals responded to chemical changes of their environment especially by alterations of species spectrum, abundance, biomass, or dominance structure: Negative long-term effects of liming; and fertilization were observed especially oil enchytraeids. Thus, the population density of Cogrtettia sphagnetorum decreased significantly tinder the influence of Iime and mineral fertilizer: Lumbricids responded to liming and fertilization with an increase of abundance and biomass (e.g., Dendrobaena rubida). The methodical basis for investigations on ecotoxicological effects of acid solutions, pesticides, or halocarbons has been the so-called photoeclector test: After contamination of the soil surface with diluted acid solutions (pH 1, pH 2) most arthropods (e.g., Collembola, Araneae, Coleoptera, Brachycera) emerged in higher numbers than on the control area. On the contrary, spraying the soil surface with concentrated acid solutions (pH 0) caused a decrease in the abundance of most arthropods (Acarina, Coleoptera, Hymenoptera, Nematocera, Brachycera). More distinct effects were observed under the influence of different pesticides and chlorocarbons. In the first 8 weeks following contamination most pesticides caused a severe decrease of the population density of various arthropods. Extremely toxic have been insecticides which influence the nervous system of arthropods (e.g., Lindan, Cypermethrin, Endosulfan). A decline of abundance was also' mainfested under the influence of trichloroacetic acid referring to Nematoda and Ciliata and under the influence of chloroform referring to Enchytraeidae and Nematocera. The tendency of essential elements to accumulate in food chains of soil invertebrates was influenced by species-specific physiological requirements and by the nutrient concentrations in the food. Mobility, chemical similarities to essential elements, and species-specific detoxification strategies are airnong the most important of influencing the accumulation of potentially toxic metals in the detritophagous food chain of the spruce stand. © 1993 Blackwell Wissenschafts-Verlag, Berlin. DA
